eByIdAsync(id) then createInstance() if they are ComponentNodes.\n\nWait — they're on different pages. Component instances can be created and appended to the workspace page. Since components live in the same file, `component.createInstance()` should work.\n\nThe instructions say \"Copy these components into your own file and use them as instances.\" Since we ARE in the file, we can just create instances directly.\n\nStrategy: Build the design with code in manageable sections:\n1. Setup: switch to workspace page, load fonts (Archivo Black, Plus Jakarta Sans - Regular, Medium, Bold, SemiBold, ExtraBold etc.)\n2. Create shared component builder helpers within the code — build header, announcement bar, newsletter, footer as reusable local components in the workspace.\n3. Build 4 frames: 01_Home, 02_Category, 03_Product, 04_Cart — each 1440 wide.\n4. Wire prototype interactions.\n\nGiven tool code size limit (50000 chars), I'll split into multiple use_figma calls. I need to be careful that state isn't persisted between calls (each call runs a fresh plugin context? Actually in Figma MCP use_figma, each call executes separately, so I need to recreate helpers each time or store IDs).\n\nPlan of execution:\n- Call A: Setup page, load fonts, create shared components (Header, Footer, Newsletter, Announcement Bar as local components in workspace), create helper functions and build the four empty frames. Actually, better: create components first, then build each frame in separate calls.\n\nLet me think about the design system:\n- Brand: #000000\n- Background: #FFFFFF\n- Surface (hero, cards): #F0EEED (light warm gray — like original SHOP.CO)\n- Text primary: #000000\n- Text secondary: rgba(0,0,0,0.6)\n- Border: rgba(0,0,0,0.1)\n- Rating star: #FFC633\n- Discount badge bg: rgba(255,51,51,0.1), text #FF3333\n- Verified: #01AB31\n- Trash/remove: #FF3333\n\nFonts: Archivo Black for wordmark + display headings (h1, section titles like \"NEW ARRIVALS\", \"TOP SELLING\", \"BROWSE BY DRESS STYLE\", \"OUR HAPPY CUSTOMERS\", \"ONE LIFE GRAPHIC T-SHIRT\", \"YOUR CART\", newsletter title). Plus Jakarta Sans for everything else.\n\nLayout: 1440 wide, content margin 100px each side (1240 content width) — matches original design.\n\nPage structure per frame (vertical flow):\n1. Announcement bar (38px, black bg, white text)\n2. Header (white, ~96px tall with padding)\n3. Content\n4. Newsletter (black card overlapping footer)\n5. Footer (light gray #F0F0F0)\n\nLet me plan each frame's heights:\n- 01_Home: announcement(38) + header(~96) + hero(~663) + brand strip(122) + new arrivals + top selling + dress style + reviews + newsletter + footer. Probably ~4000px.\n- 02_Category: filters + grid → ~1900px + footer\n- 03_Product: gallery + summary + tabs + reviews + also like → ~3000px\n- 04_Cart: ~1200px\n\nI'll use auto-layout heavily for maintainability.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll write a shared \"library\" call that creates components and stores their IDs, then separate calls per screen. But since each call is independent, I can't share JS functions between calls. I'll need to re-include helpers in each call. To minimize, I'll create the components in call 1, then in subsequent calls use `figma.getNodeByIdAsync` to find them and create instances.\n\nImportant: instances of components on another page: allowed — instance can be appended to any page.\n\nLet me also consider: creating instances of Button (1:23) and Text Field (1:26). The Text Field has a label etc. Let me check what the Button and Text Field look like — screenshot them first.\n\nLet me take screenshots of the components page and media page to understand what they look like.\n\nActually, let me first screenshot 1:23 (Button) and 1:26 (Text Field), and maybe the whole components frame 1:19.\n\nAlso need to check fonts available. Archivo Black and Plus Jakarta Sans are Google fonts, should be available in Figma.\n\nLet me plan the code structure.\n\nCall 1: Create a \"🧩 Components\" section on workspace page with reusable components:\n- C_AnnouncementBar\n- C_Header (with variants? simpler: one component, set current nav via instance swap... but current state differs: Shop current on 02/03, none on 01/04. I could make the header a component and manually adjust instances. Or create two components. Simplest: one component; for current state, I'll just not use underline in component and add underline rectangle in instances where needed. Hmm, but modifying instance internals is limited. Let me make the nav items individually as components? That's overkill.\n\nAlternative approach: build header as a component with all elements; for \"current: Shop\", I can show a current indicator. Since text properties can be overridden in instances (font weight etc. can be changed on instance text nodes). Actually yes — instance text nodes can have font overrides. But adding an underline requires inserting a node, which is not allowed in instances. I could include the underline in the component as a hidden node, and toggle visibility in instances. visibility overrides are allowed. \n\nPlan for Header component:\n- container: 1440 x 96ish, white bg, bottom border\n- SHOP.CO wordmark (Archivo Black 32)\n- nav: Shop + chevron down, On Sale, New Arrivals, Brands — each nav item is a frame with text + hidden underline bar\n- search field: rounded 62px, bg #F0F0F0, search icon + placeholder \"Search for products...\"\n- cart icon, account icon\n\nFor Shop current state: make \"Shop\" text bold + underline visible.\n\nI'll create the header component once with underline hidden; in instances for 02/03, set underline visible and font weight bold.\n\nSimilarly for other components:\n- C_Footer (full 1440 wide)\n- C_Newsletter (1240 wide black card with radius 20)\n- C_ProductCard: photo instance + name + rating + price. This varies a lot per product. Instead of one rigid component, I'll create a helper function `makeProductCard(...)` in code that builds each card and creates a component