onents matters. I'll create components for the repeated elements and use instances.\n\nLet me structure:\n\nCall 1: Setup workspace page; load fonts; create components section with:\n- announcementBarComp\n- headerComp  \n- footerComp\n- newsletterComp\n- productCardComp\n- reviewCardComp\n- styleTileComp\n- Return component IDs.\n\nCall 2: Build 01_Home frame using instances.\nCall 3: Build 02_Category.\nCall 4: Build 03_Product.\nCall 5: Build 04_Cart.\nCall 6: Wire prototype.\nCall 7+: Screenshots and fixes.\n\nEach call's code must be self-contained (helpers re-declared). Let me define helper functions compactly.\n\nCommon helpers needed per call:\n```js\nasync function font(family, style){ await figma.loadFontAsync({family, style}); }\nconst AB = {family:\"Archivo Black\", style:\"Regular\"};\nconst PJ = (w)=>({family:\"Plus Jakarta Sans\", style:w});\n```\nPlus Jakarta Sans styles: \"Regular\", \"Medium\", \"SemiBold\"? Actually Figma lists Plus Jakarta Sans with styles: ExtraLight, Light, Regular, Medium, SemiBold, Bold, ExtraBold. I believe \"SemiBold\" (one word). Let me load: Regular, Medium, SemiBold, Bold, ExtraBold. And Archivo Black only has \"Regular\".\n\nText helper:\n```js\nasync function txt(str, {f=PJ(\"Regular\"), size=16, color=[0,0,0], opacity=1, lh, ls, align} = {}) {\n  await figma.loadFontAsync(f);\n  const t = figma.createText();\n  t.fontName = f;\n  t.characters = str;\n  t.fontSize = size;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nInstance helper: `async function inst(id){ const c = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(id); const i = c.createInstance(); return i; }`\n\nColors:\n- black [0,0,0]\n- white [1,1,1]\n- surface #F0EEED → [0.941,0.933,0.929]\n- footer bg #F0F0F0\n- textSecondary: black 60%\n- star #FFC633 → [1,0.776,0.2]\n- discount text #FF3333, bg #FF333310\n- verified #01AB31 → [0.004,0.671,0.192]\n\nLet me now write Call 1. I'll create a frame \"Components · SHOP.CO\" in workspace at some x offset, containing the components laid out.\n\nComponent details:\n\n**C_AnnouncementBar** (1440x38): black bg, centered text \"Sign up and get 20% off to your first order. Sign Up Now\" — \"Sign Up Now\" is underlined + medium. Two text nodes: plain + link. Close icon at right (x=1310). Layout: use absolute positioning for simplicity inside a fixed-size frame. I'll place:\n- container frame 1440x38, fill black\n- text group centered horizontally: t1 \"Sign up and get 20% off to your first order.\" (white, 14), t2 \"Sign Up Now\" (white, underline, medium 14)\n- close icon instance at right, recolored white.\n\nIcon recolor: instances of icon components — can I override fills of children? Yes, on instance children you can set fills? For instance sublayers, you can override fill via `node.fills` on the vector inside instance? Overriding paint of nested vector in an instance is allowed (fills is an overridable property). Let me just try: get instance, find vector child, set fills. I think it works.\n\n**C_Header** (1440x96): white bg, bottom stroke rgba(0,0,0,0.1).\n- wordmark \"SHOP.CO\" Archivo Black 32 at x=100, y=32\n- nav at x=321: Shop(+chevron), On Sale, New Arrivals, Brands; gap 24; each 16px regular\n- search: 577 wide x 48, radius 62, bg #F0F0F0, icon search 24 + placeholder text\n- icons: cart (24), account (24) at right, gap 14\nLet me compute: content width 1240 (100 margins). wordmark ~160 wide. nav group ~330. search flex 1. \n\nI'll use auto-layout for header: horizontal, padding 100px 0... Actually padding: horizontal 100, vertical 24. itemSpacing 40. Inside: wordmark, nav group (spacing 24), search (layoutGrow 1), icons group (spacing 14).\n\nNav item component: frame horizontal spacing 4: text + chevron icon (only Shop has chevron). For \"current\" I'll add an underline: hmm, auto-layout with hidden underline bar... The underline as separate absolutely positioned node inside a wrapper frame. Simpler: nav item = vertical autolayout: row(text+chevron), underline rect (2px, full width, hidden). Hidden items don't take space in auto-layout. \n\nI'll make a mini component C_NavItem with props? Component properties are complex; just create instances and override text + visibility. But chevron only for Shop: include chevron hidden by default, show for Shop instance.\n\nActually simpler: I'll create the nav items inline in header (not as components) — 4 groups each with text node; Shop group includes chevron instance + hidden underline. For current state override: set Shop text Bold + underline visible in instances of header.\n\nFor header as component, override visibility of underline inside instance: allowed (visible is overridable).\n\n**C_Footer** (1440 wide, height ~500 incl. newsletter overlap?). Newsletter overlaps footer in original design (newsletter card sits half over footer). To keep components simple: Footer component = 1440 x ~410, bg #F0F0F0, with top padding ~140 (space for newsletter overlap handled by page layout: newsletter positioned absolute above footer). Hmm — if footer is inside a page auto-layout, overlapping needs absolute positioning. In Figma auto-layout, children can be absolutely positioned (absolutePosition in auto-layout frame = \"absolute\" positioning). Yes! Auto-layout supports absolutely-positioned children.\n\nPlan: each page frame = vertical auto-layout: announcement, header, content sections..., footerWrap (relative container with footer + newsletter absolute above). \n\nSimplest: page sections stacked via auto-layout, and for the newsletter overlap: make \"footerSection\" an auto-layout frame containing footer; then add newsletter as absolutely positioned child centered horizontally, top = -90 (overlap). That works: absolutely positioned children can have negative offsets? position via x,y relative to parent. I'll set newsletter.x = 100, newsletter.y = -90ish after appending. Newsletter height ~180, so footer needs top padding of ~90+ for the bottom half of newsletter to sit inside footer top area... Actually in original: newsletter card straddles the boundary — top half on white background, bottom half on footer bg. So: place footer as last child; then add newsletter absolutely at y = -(newsletterHeight/2) relative to footer? If newsletter is child of footer frame, y=-90 places it half above. And footer top padding must account: footer content starts at y≈140 within footer so it doesn't collide with the newsletter bottom half. Wait the newsletter overlaps footer's top edge: newsletter bottom = footer top + 90. So footer content must start below y=90+... no: newsletter occupies from -90 to +90 relative to footer top. Footer content (logo etc.) starts at ~y=140 inside footer. \n\nI'll make footer component include empty top space of 140px. Footer total height ~ 140 + content(~280) + bottom bar.\n\nFooter content:\n- Row: left col: SHOP.CO wordmark (Archivo 28?), description (14, secondary, max width ~250), social icons row (4 icons in white circles with border).\n- 4 link columns: COMPANY, HELP, FAQ, RESOURCES — each title (16, medium, letterspaced uppercase, black) + 4 links (16, secondary 60%).\n- Divider line\n- Bottom row: \"Shop.co © 2000-2023, All Rights Reserved\" left; payment logos right (5 logos in white rounded cards).\n\nPayment logos: instances, sized ~46x30 each in white card 46x30? In original they're in white rounded rectangles ~53x33. I'll wrap each instance in a white rounded rect with padding.\n\nSocial icons: white circle 28x28 with border, icon instance ~14 inside (colored black). Twitter/Facebook/Instagram/GitHub.\n\n**C_Newsletter** (1240 x 180): black bg, radius 20. Left: \"STAY UP TO DATE ABOUT OUR LATEST OFFERS\" Archivo Black 40 white, two lines, width ~600. Right: email field (349x48, radius 62, white bg, gray icon+text \"Enter your email address\") + button (349x48 white bg radius 62, black text \"Subscribe to Newsletter\", medium). Use instance of Text Field? The given Text Field component (1:26) — let me check its design first with a screenshot. Its width is 400x76, probabl